  <h2>Open source SCADA: the source is public, and you can read all of it</h2>
  <p>Most people who search for open source SCADA want two things: no license meter running, and code they can read when something breaks at 3am. HotLoop's source is public under the HotLoop Source License Agreement 1.0, home and lab use is free, and <a href="/license/">the license page says which terms apply to you</a>.</p>

  <h2>Cheap SCADA software that starts on a shelf</h2>
  <p><a href="/iot/">HotLoop IoT</a> runs on a Raspberry Pi, installs with no login and no license key, and keeps your devices, tags and history when you move up to <a href="/edge/">Edge</a> or the <a href="/gateway/">Gateway</a>. You buy hardware once and grow into the site, with no migration weekend.</p>

  <h2>Ignition SCADA alternatives, or Ignition and HotLoop side by side</h2>
  <p>Running Ignition already? The Gateway reads Ignition's OPC UA server and serves its own, and Ignition reads it end to end, so the HotLoop box at the machine feeds the SCADA you already own. Not running it? The Gateway runs a whole site from one box. <a href="/opc-ua-gateway/">See the OPC UA gateway page</a>.</p>
